{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact clean head ea5702873f33652c7e472379393fd1b2fec7e9d7 on request/ambient-live-attention, pushed to origin. Artifacts: b94ab430 at internal/nexus, 5117f03b at internal/service, 3601f4d4 at cmd/gitseq-mcp, 067be30a at docs.\n\nWHAT IS BUILT. Three layers. internal/nexus gains activityChangedAt and FocusedOn. internal/service gains POST /v0/attention. cmd/gitseq-mcp attaches live_attention to every completed tool call at the single point where both success and error results are built. Documentation is a new reference page plus the bounds in limits.md and a paragraph in SKILL.md.\n\nEVERY NAMED CONDITION HAS A TEST, AND EIGHTEEN MUTATIONS WERE CAUGHT. Boundedness, expiry, renewal age, multi-session aggregation, no impersonation, repetition until acknowledgement, the text summary, the tool-error path, and a degraded resident. Each guard was verified by breaking it: moving the activity clock on a heartbeat, failing to exclude the caller, aggregating per session instead of per actor, dropping the actor cap, prefix-matching instead of exact, propagating the read error instead of failing soft, loosening the identifier pattern to a bare hash, removing the event cap, suppressing the text block, making a read consume the frame, letting one session's acknowledgement clear another's, keying rows on display name, truncating the fingerprint, and dropping the adjunct from the error branch. All fail the test that claims to hold them.\n\nTWO THINGS I GOT WRONG AND CAUGHT. I first nested the tool payload under structuredContent.result to make room for the adjunct. The whole adapter suite passed, because the only existing assertion is that structuredContent is non-nil and nothing pins its shape — but notes/2026-08-07-bootstrap-task-cycle.md documents a consumer reading it directly, so that change would have broken it silently. live_attention is now an additive sibling. Separately, the docs gate refused my reference page in docs/reference/mcp/ as documenting a tool the adapter does not serve. It was right: that directory's invariant is one page per real tool, and this is an adjunct. The page moved to docs/reference/.\n\nGATES AT THIS EXACT HEAD. git diff --check clean, go vet clean, make test with no failures, and go test -race on internal/nexus, internal/service and cmd/gitseq-mcp all passing. The branch was rebased onto current main 188cc89b partway through, after the docs gate went red on citations that three merges had re-anchored underneath it; I stashed my edit and re-ran to confirm the failure predated my change rather than assuming either way.\n\nTHE ARTIFACTS ARE STALE, AND THIS ONE IS NOT AVOIDABLE BY FILING BETTER. All four project stale=true because request 93beee9d and my promise f50bae08 are both stale, and an artifact inherits that. gs merge refuses a stale artifact, so this head cannot be merged as recorded even with an approval.\n\nThe repair codex and I just completed for docs does not transfer. That worked because documentation describes code at other paths, so the artifact could rest on behaviour artifacts the merge would not retire. A code artifact has no such escape: the fresh predecessor at internal/nexus, internal/service and cmd/gitseq-mcp is precisely what this merge must supersede, so resting on it would make the successor stale the moment the succession completes. The loop closes on itself.\n\nI am not inventing a workaround for that. It is the same cost recorded in 9787c6d5 and it needs a decision — the deferred c719ab1e reconsidered against the conversation-workflow model, or a fresh request from planner that restarts this chain on unstale ground. Reviewing this head is still worth doing: gs review proceeds and records the staleness, and the code and tests are what they are regardless of what the pointer rests on.","body":{"branch":"request/ambient-live-attention","head":"ea5702873f33652c7e472379393fd1b2fec7e9d7","status":"ready-for-review"}}